id=a52sxq_codec_av1_sw_off
name=Galaxy A52s 5G: no software AV1 decoders in the codec list
author=kusti420
description=The Snapdragon 778G decodes H.264/HEVC/VP9/MPEG-2 in hardware (c2.qti.*) but has no AV1 block; the only AV1 decoders are the software ones of the Google swcodec APEX, c2.android.av1-dav1d.decoder and c2.android.av1.decoder (gav1), and any app that uses the platform codec list (ExoPlayer/Media3: YouTube, TikTok, Samsung Internet, Samsung Video Player, Gallery) picks AV1 when the server offers it. Measured 2026-10 (/mnt/B1/OneUi9/scratch/battery_social/REPORT.md): 1080p30 AV1 = 315 mA vs 262-265 mA H.264/VP9 (+20 %), 1080p60 AV1 = 377 mA (+44 %), media.swcodec at 48-83 % of a core. This patch installs /product/etc/media_codecs_c2.xml, which Codec2InfoBuilder parses after the APEX media_codecs.xml and before the vendor list (search order /product/etc, /odm/etc, /vendor/etc, /system/etc; the name exists in no other partition so nothing is shadowed): two update="true" enabled="false" entries, so both AV1 decoders are dropped from the MediaCodecList and apps get the VP9/H.264 hardware decoders instead. Verified live 2026-11-04 on 9.3.105 with the file bind-mounted over /product/etc and mediaserver restarted: dumpsys media.player lists no decoder under video/av01 any more (only c2.android.av1.encoder), the parser logs "disabling c2.android.av1-dav1d.decoder" / "disabling c2.android.av1.decoder", and the Samsung Video Player on a 1080p30 AV1 mp4 logs "libsfextcp: can't find a codec for video/av01" + "MediaCodecOutputGenerator: initCodec failed to create proper codec, mime video/av01" and stays up (no crash, audio track still opens). Trade-off: a file or stream that is AV1-only cannot be played by platform players (Samsung Video Player, Gallery, Samsung Internet HTML5 video on AV1-only sources: unsupported-format, no crash); YouTube/TikTok/Netflix fall back to VP9/H.264 (Netflix streams AV1 only to devices it certified for it), Instagram Reels and Chrome's own media stack bundle dav1d and are unaffected either way. The Google APEX itself is untouched (Play system updates keep applying); a Mainline swcodec update cannot restore AV1 because the parse order lives in /system's libsfplugin_ccodec and the override is applied after whatever APEX media_codecs.xml is active - unless a future APEX renames the two components, in which case the update entries become a logged no-op (MediaCodecsXmlParser "cannot update non-existing codec"). A user who wants AV1 back can hide the file (KernelSU: bind-mount a copy of /product/etc without it; the list is rebuilt when mediaserver restarts). Log check: logcat MediaCodecsXmlParser "disabling c2.android.av1-dav1d.decoder" / "disabling c2.android.av1.decoder" when mediaserver builds the list.
